recover the trapped syscall number from siginfo on x86

Android's zygote seccomp policy for apps blocks the plain rename()
syscall on 64-bit architectures (SECCOMP_ALLOWLIST_APP.TXT marks
rename lp32-only), so any glibc program inside the proot rootfs that
calls rename() - apt's FileFd::Rename, dpkg, coreutils mv - receives
SIGSYS.  proot's SIGSYS handler already downgrades such syscalls to
allowed equivalents (PR_rename -> PR_renameat), but on x86_64 it never
got the chance: it recovered the syscall number from rax after
rewinding orig_rax, and by the time the SIGSYS is observed, proot's own
handling of the syscall-exit stop that seccomp emits right before the
signal has already consumed rax as a result value.  Both orig_rax and
rax read 0, which decodes as PR_read, matches no downgrade case, and
falls into the default branch that returns -ENOSYS - the exact
"rename failed, Function not implemented" that killed apt in the
Ubuntu E2E run.

The siginfo of a SYS_SECCOMP SIGSYS reports the trapped syscall number
in si_syscall; it is the only reliable source at that point.  Pass it
from the SIGSYS case in event.c into handle_seccomp_event() and use it
to restore both the syscall-number and result register on x86.
arm/arm64 keep the old register-based recovery: their syscall number
lives in a register that is not the result register, so the number
survives.  (This is why the fork never saw the bug on its own arm64
devices.)

Validated on x86_64 Linux with a seccomp filter that TRAPs __NR_rename
exactly like the Android app policy: rename() under the filter now
downgrades to renameat and succeeds; a trapped-but-unmapped syscall
(syslog) still returns ENOSYS; untrapped syscalls are unaffected.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; this patch is applied to
the pinned fork tarball by fetchLinuxSource in linux/build.gradle.kts.
--- a/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
+++ b/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
@@ -71,7 +71,7 @@
  *
  * Return 0 to swallow signal or SIGSYS to deliver it to process.
  */
-int handle_seccomp_event(Tracee* tracee)
+int handle_seccomp_event(Tracee* tracee, int trapped_syscall)
 {
 	int ret;
 
@@ -92,13 +92,34 @@
 	/* Save regs so they can be restored at end of replaced call.  */
 	save_current_regs(tracee, ORIGINAL_SECCOMP_REWRITE);
 
-	/* X86 uses orig_rax when selecting syscall,
-	 * however at this point we are after syscall has been rejected
-	 * and orig_rax was reset to -1.  */
+	/* X86 uses orig_rax when selecting syscall, however at this point we
+	 * are after syscall has been rejected and orig_rax was reset to -1.
+	 * The kernel rolls the result register back to the syscall number
+	 * (syscall_rollback), but PRoot's own handling of the syscall-exit
+	 * stop that seccomp emits right before the SIGSYS can already have
+	 * consumed rax as a syscall result, losing the number there too
+	 * (observed: both orig_rax and rax equal 0, i.e. PR_read).  The
+	 * siginfo of a SYS_SECCOMP SIGSYS reports the trapped syscall number
+	 * (si_syscall) and is the only reliable source, so prefer it.  */
 #if defined(ARCH_X86_64)
-	tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_rax = tracee->_regs[CURRENT].rax;
+	if (trapped_syscall >= 0) {
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_rax = (unsigned long long) trapped_syscall;
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].rax = (unsigned long long) trapped_syscall;
+	} else {
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_rax = tracee->_regs[CURRENT].rax;
+	}
 #elif defined(ARCH_X86)
-	tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_eax = tracee->_regs[CURRENT].eax;
+	if (trapped_syscall >= 0) {
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_eax = (unsigned long long) trapped_syscall;
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].eax = (unsigned long long) trapped_syscall;
+	} else {
+		tracee->_regs[CURRENT].orig_eax = tracee->_regs[CURRENT].eax;
+	}
+#else
+	/* Other architectures keep the syscall number in a register that
+	 * is not the result register, so the fallback below is reliable
+	 * there.  */
+	(void) trapped_syscall;
 #endif
 
 	print_current_regs(tracee, 3, "seccomp SIGSYS");
